\title{The Zero-Dimensional Persistent Homology of the Prime Point Cloud:
Unbounded Betti Growth and the Absence of a Global Merge Scale}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the zero-dimensional persistent homology of the \emph{prime point cloud},
the sequence of prime numbers regarded as a point cloud on the real line. For points
on a line the Vietoris--Rips filtration is completely transparent: at resolution
$\varepsilon$ two consecutive points belong to the same connected component precisely
when the gap between them is at most $\varepsilon$. This yields an exact Betti staircase
for the zeroth Betti number in terms of the prime gap sequence, and a barcode whose bar
lengths are exactly the prime gaps. Building on this finite-$n$ description, we analyze
the asymptotics as the number of points $n \to \infty$ at a fixed resolution
$\varepsilon$. Our central result is that the prime Betti curve
$n \mapsto \beta_0(\varepsilon, n)$ tends to $+\infty$ for every fixed
$\varepsilon \ge 0$: the prime cloud shatters into arbitrarily many connected
components at every scale, and there is no global merge scale at which the infinite
prime cloud is connected. The phenomenon is driven by a single elementary arithmetic
input --- the unboundedness of prime gaps, via Euclid's factorial construction of
arbitrarily long runs of consecutive composites --- strengthened to an ``infinitely
often'' statement. We also show the total persistence of the prime barcode diverges.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Topological data analysis (TDA) equips a finite metric space with a multiscale
topological summary. Its workhorse, \emph{persistent homology}, records how homological
features --- connected components ($H_0$), loops ($H_1$), voids ($H_2$), and higher
analogues --- appear and disappear as a scale parameter $\varepsilon$ increases. The
output is a \emph{barcode}, a stable and informative fingerprint of the data with
applications from structural biology to cosmology to materials science.

The primes $2, 3, 5, 7, 11, \dots$ form perhaps the most studied sequence in
mathematics, yet their geometry as a point cloud is rarely examined through the TDA
lens. This paper does exactly that in dimension zero, where the theory is completely
explicit and connects directly to the classical theory of prime gaps.

Throughout, let $p_i$ denote the $i$-th prime with $p_0 = 2$, $p_1 = 3$, $p_2 = 5$,
and define the \emph{prime point cloud} to be the sequence $P = (p_i)_{i \ge 0}$
regarded as points on $\R$. Our contributions are: (1) a clean use of the Betti
staircase for $H_0$ of a point cloud on a line, specialized to the primes; (2) an
``infinitely often'' version of the unboundedness of prime gaps; (3) the main
asymptotic theorem, that at every fixed resolution the zeroth Betti number of the prime
cloud tends to infinity and is monotone in $n$; and (4) the no-global-merge corollary
and the divergence of total persistence.

\section{Definitions and the zero-dimensional barcode on a line}

\subsection{The Vietoris--Rips filtration and $H_0$}
Let $X = \{x_0, \dots, x_{n-1}\} \subset \R$ be finite. For $\varepsilon \ge 0$, form
the graph $G_\varepsilon(X)$ on $X$ with an edge between $x$ and $y$ whenever
$|x - y| \le \varepsilon$. The zeroth Betti number $\beta_0(\varepsilon)$ is the number
of connected components of $G_\varepsilon(X)$, equivalently the rank of $H_0$ of the
Vietoris--Rips complex at scale $\varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon$ increases the graph only
gains edges, so components only merge; this makes $\{G_\varepsilon(X)\}$ a filtration
and its homology a persistence module.

\subsection{The Betti staircase}
Assume the points are sorted, $x_0 < x_1 < \dots < x_{n-1}$, with gaps
$g_i = x_{i+1} - x_i$. On a line, two points lie in the same component of
$G_\varepsilon(X)$ if and only if every gap between them is $\le \varepsilon$; a
component boundary occurs exactly at each gap of size $> \varepsilon$. Counting
boundaries yields the \emph{Betti staircase}:
\begin{equation}
\beta_0(\varepsilon, n) = 1 + \#\{\, i < n : g_i > \varepsilon \,\}.
\end{equation}
As a function of $\varepsilon$ this is a non-increasing step function; as a function of
$n$ it is non-decreasing.

\subsection{Barcode and total persistence}
For points on a line the $H_0$ barcode is simple: each point is born as its own
component at $\varepsilon = 0$, and components merge one at a time as $\varepsilon$
passes each gap value. With the convention that the younger component dies at a merge,
each finite bar has length equal to exactly one gap $g_i$, plus one infinite bar. The
total persistence telescopes:
\begin{equation}
\TP(n) = \sum_{i=0}^{n-2} g_i = x_{n-1} - x_0.
\end{equation}
For the prime cloud with $x_0 = p_0 = 2$, the total persistence over the first $n$
points equals $p_n - 2$ in the indexing used below.

\subsection{Prime-specific notation}
Specializing to $X = \{p_0, \dots, p_{n-1}\}$, the gaps are the \emph{prime gaps}
$g_i = p_{i+1} - p_i$, and the prime Betti staircase reads
\begin{equation}
\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) = 1 + \#\{\, i < n : p_{i+1} - p_i > \varepsilon \,\}.
\end{equation}

\section{Unboundedness of prime gaps, infinitely often}

\begin{lemma}[Composite run]\label{lem:run}
For all integers $N$ and $j$ with $2 \le j \le N$, the number $N! + j$ is composite.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $2 \le j \le N$, the factor $j$ appears in $N! = 1 \cdot 2 \cdots N$, so
$j \mid N!$. Also $j \mid j$, hence $j \mid (N! + j)$. Now $1 < j$ and $j < N! + j$
(as $N! \ge 1$), so $j$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N! + j$, which is therefore not
prime.
\end{proof}

Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:run} to $j = 2, \dots, N$ produces the $N-1$ consecutive
composites $N!+2, \dots, N!+N$: a prime desert of length $N-1$. Since $N$ is arbitrary,
deserts of every length exist.

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded gaps, infinitely often]\label{thm:gaps}
For every bound $B$ and every index $M$ there exists an index $n \ge M$ with
$g_n = p_{n+1} - p_n > B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $N$ large enough that $N > B$ and $N!+2$ lies beyond the $M$-th prime; e.g.
$N = B + p_M + 2$, using $N \le N!$. Let $n$ be the index of the largest prime below
$N!+2$; since $N!+2 > p_M$, we have $n \ge M$. The next prime $p_{n+1}$ cannot lie in
the desert $\{N!+2, \dots, N!+N\}$, so $p_{n+1} \ge N!+N+1$ while $p_n < N!+2$. Hence
$g_n > (N!+N+1) - (N!+2) = N-1 \ge B$ after absorbing the constant into the choice of
$N$. The threshold is exceeded at $n \ge M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Infinitude of wide gaps]\label{cor:inf}
For every $c$, the index set $\{\, n : g_n > c \,\}$ is infinite.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Given any $a$, Theorem~\ref{thm:gaps} with $B = c$, $M = a+1$ produces $n > a$ with
$g_n > c$. Thus the set has elements above every $a$ and is infinite.
\end{proof}

\section{Growth of the prime Betti curve}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in $n$]\label{prop:mono}
For any point sequence and any fixed $\varepsilon$, $n \mapsto \beta_0(\varepsilon, n)$
is non-decreasing.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By the staircase, $\beta_0(\varepsilon, n) = 1 + \#\{ i < n : g_i > \varepsilon\}$;
increasing $n$ enlarges the index range, so the count only grows.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Wide-gap counts are unbounded]\label{prop:count}
For every threshold $c$ and every $M$ there exists $n$ with
$\#\{\, i < n : g_i > c \,\} \ge M$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $M$; the case $M=0$ is trivial. Given $n$ realizing count $\ge M$, apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:gaps} with bound $c$ and start index $n$ to obtain $m \ge n$ with
$g_m > c$. This index is not yet counted, so adjoining it and passing to range $m+1$
raises the count to $\ge M+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime Betti curve is unbounded]\label{thm:unb}
For every $\varepsilon \ge 0$ and every $M$ there exists $n$ with
$\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) \ge M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $c = \lceil \varepsilon \rceil$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:count} there is $n$ with
at least $M$ indices $i < n$ satisfying $g_i > c \ge \varepsilon$; each also satisfies
$g_i > \varepsilon$, so $\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) \ge 1 + M \ge M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime shattering]\label{thm:shatter}
For every fixed $\varepsilon \ge 0$,
$\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) \to +\infty$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The curve is monotone (Proposition~\ref{prop:mono}) and unbounded above
(Theorem~\ref{thm:unb}); a monotone, unbounded-above integer sequence tends to
$+\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No global merge scale]\label{cor:merge}
For every $\varepsilon$ there exists $n$ with $\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) > 1$: the
infinite prime cloud is not connected at any fixed scale.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unb} with $M = 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Divergence of total persistence}

\begin{theorem}[Total persistence diverges]\label{thm:tp}
The total persistence of the prime barcode over the first $n$ points equals $p_n - 2$,
and hence $\TP(n) \to +\infty$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By telescoping, $\TP(n) = \sum_i g_i = p_n - p_0 = p_n - 2$. The primes are strictly
increasing and unbounded, so $p_n \to \infty$ and $p_n - 2 \to \infty$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion and future work}

\textbf{Rate of shattering.} Theorem~\ref{thm:shatter} gives divergence but not a rate.
Prime Number Theorem heuristics predict that for fixed $\varepsilon$ a positive
proportion of gaps eventually exceed $\varepsilon$, giving a linear lower bound
$\beta_0^{P}(\varepsilon, n) = \Omega(n)$. Even a weak explicit lower bound via the
average gap $p_n/n \to \infty$ would quantify the shattering.

\textbf{The twin bar.} The shortest finite bar has length $2$, corresponding to a gap
$g_i = 2$, i.e. twin primes. Whether infinitely many length-$2$ bars occur is exactly
the Twin Prime Conjecture, recast as a statement about the shortest bars of the prime
barcode --- dual to the largest-gap analysis here.

\textbf{Higher dimensions.} Placing the primes on a line makes $H_1$ and above trivial.
Embeddings in $\R^2$ or higher (Ulam spiral, residue coordinates) would activate higher
persistent homology and pose new questions.

\section{Conclusion}

Viewed as a point cloud on the line, the primes have a completely computable
zero-dimensional persistent homology: the Betti number is a staircase in the prime
gaps, and the bar lengths are the prime gaps. From the elementary fact that gaps are
unbounded infinitely often, we conclude that at every fixed resolution the prime cloud
fractures into arbitrarily many components, that no global scale renders the infinite
cloud connected, and that total persistence diverges. Arithmetic divergence and
topological divergence are one and the same.

\end{document}

